.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt DATE 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm date
.Nd print software civil time and its source quality
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl u
.Op Cm +%s
.Op Fl -status
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the boot-local software civil clock in UTC, to whole seconds.
.Fl u
is accepted and changes nothing; time zones and other formats are not provided.
.Cm +%s
prints Unix seconds instead.
.Fl -status
prints source, whether a value is known, staleness, successful-sample age in
monotonic ticks, correction generation, last attempt source/error and the last
successful transport RTT and successful sample tick. It also shows the raw
RTC reading and signed civil-minus-RTC nanoseconds from adjacent samples. The
RTC has whole-second resolution and the reads are not simultaneous; divergence
is observable, not a measurement of UTC accuracy. Absolute uncertainty remains
unknown. RTC and operator assertions are explicitly unsynchronized; an expired
network estimate is stale. A current unauthenticated estimate is labeled as
such, without an accuracy claim.
.Pp
The clock starts from an RTC reading between the fixed 2026 build floor and
2099, or unknown. Status names a before-build-floor refusal; raw RTC reads
remain unchanged. An explicit operator assertion can still select 1970..2099.
Its initial eligible RTC source is
rtc-unverified: the RTC is assumed UTC, not authenticated or measured accurate.
An authenticated SSH or enrolled local operator can use
.Xr clock 1
to assert time, or explicitly configure
.Xr time.conf 5
for unauthenticated SNTP estimates. The first network sample can replace an
eligible RTC anchor without the step bound; later network corrections relative
to a prior network value or operator assertion obey the per-correction bound.
An explicit operator assertion itself is not step-bounded.
There is no cumulative correction limit across intervals. Such an estimate
is not trusted UTC.
Failed synchronization retains the prior successful source and separately
reports the failure; an expired network estimate is labeled stale.
.Pp
Corrections affect only the software civil anchor, including ordinary file
modification times and log collection. Monotonic deadlines, uptime, RT budgets,
watchdogs and network timeouts do not change. A correction generation invalidates the FSD clock cache before its next
operation; ordinary time passage retains the bounded refresh budget. File times may
go backwards after an intentional backward step.
.Pp
Changes last only this boot and do not write the RTC. A reboot initializes from
RTC again. Old prebuilt applications using syscall30 continue reading raw RTC;
the new paired runtime uses software civil time. On an older kernel without the
new call, ordinary time can fall back to unverified raw RTC without quality or
cross-process correction claims; malformed new records never cause fallback.
TLS consumers with an explicit hardware-RTC/anchor policy retain that policy.
.Pp
When civil time is unavailable, only Rust
.Ql SystemTime::now
returns Unix epoch plus its monotonic uptime coordinate so the program can
continue. This bootstrap coordinate is not civil time.
.Nm ,
C wall-clock calls and FSD timestamps retain unknown/refused behavior; a file
changed in that window gets mtime 0 (unknown), never the bootstrap date.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when time/status is unavailable, 2 on invalid arguments.
Unknown time is never replaced by an invented date.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clock 1 ,
.Xr time.conf 5 ,
.Xr uptime 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7
